@startuml SandboxBackend
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Sandbox backend — first-class for claudecode + cursor;\ndocker+egress stays as the fallback path
' ── Authoritative sbx references. The Kit spec is the source of truth for every
' field below. Deriving the shape from sbx's rejection messages instead produced a
' Kit that validated while omitting what actually makes an agent session work.
'   kit spec v2 : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/blob/main/spec/SPEC-v2.md
'   kit examples: https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kit-examples/
'   spec package: https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/spec
'   contrib kits: https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib
' Kit schema, the validate handoff, session lifecycle and exit-code handling:
'   sbx-kit-contract.puml (same directory)

component "run.Do backend selection\n""man.IsSbx() && mode != review""\n&& ""internal/sbx.Available()""" as SEL <<app>>
component "internal/sbx\nAvailable · WriteKit · SecretSet\nRunArgs · RemoveArgs" as SBX <<app>>
component "sbx CLI (pre-GA; proveo pins\n""sbx.MinVersion"")\nrun --kit … / secret set / rm" as CLI <<host>>
component "Kit spec.yaml (per run)\nallowedDomains = manifest hosts ∪\nprovider endpoints ∪ PROVEO_EGRESS_PROVIDER_DOMAINS" as KIT <<app>>
component "host-side credential injection\nvalues via stdin — never argv,\nnever env, never the VM fs" as SEC <<error>>

component "docker+egress orchestration\nnets · Squid · MITM inspector" as FB <<host>>

SEL ARROW_MAIN SBX : available
SEL ARROW_QUEUE FB : unavailable → one actionable warn
SBX ARROW_MAIN CLI
SBX ..> KIT : rendered under state egDir/sbx/kit
SBX ..> SEC

note right of SEL
  **Auto w/ fallback, one operator override.** Harnesses with
  ""docker: sbx"" (""claudecode"", ""cursor"") take the sandbox backend
  whenever the host supports it. The choice prompt carries it as the
  CHECKED add-on ""docker (sandbox)"" — unchecking is the one way to send
  such a run down docker+egress instead, and the answer is remembered
  per harness like every other axis. Unsupported hosts
  (no sbx on PATH, or no Apple Silicon / KVM) fall back to docker+egress
  with a single warn naming the reason. ""--print"" renders whichever
  backend was selected (""sbx run …"" vs ""docker run ..."").
end note

note right of SBX
  **Availability gate** (all must hold):
  | sbx CLI on PATH
  | darwin/arm64, **or** linux with ""/dev/kvm""
  Win11/Hyper-V and Intel macs are not gated in yet.
  The review tier is greyed out in the picker when this backend is
  selected — the consent-gate socket has no sbx transport yet.
end note

note bottom of SEC
  **Credential parity with the broker, different mechanism.** Declared secret
  env vars plus every detected provider key var are injected via ""sbx secret
  set"" before the run; the sentinel/broker-file machinery of the egress path
  is deliberately not involved — deny-by-default egress replaces interception.
  Accepted gap: no DLP content scan on payloads to *allowed* hosts.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **A Kit's credentials are approved by the operator, and that is a gate rather
  than a fault.** sbx lists every credential a Kit requests — service,
  destination, whether it is stored — and waits on //"[A]pprove all · [R]eview
  each · [N]o"//. A ""proveo run"" is interactive by construction (the choice form
  precedes it, ""isStdinTTY"" gates the prompts, the agent needs a PTY), so this
  sits naturally in front of it and the e2e probe answers it the way an operator
  would.

  //An earlier note here called it a blocker.// That conflated two different
  things. ""secret set"" asking "Overwrite? (y/N)" IS a fault, because the value is
  piped on stdin and the question consumes it — the write cancels and the agent
  keeps a stale credential, silently. That is what ""--force"" fixes, and the probe
  still fails on it. Being asked a question is not the same as having an answer
  stolen.

  It does raise the cost of over-declaring, though. Provider collection is
  filtered by the manifest's ""capabilities.providers"": unfiltered, a claudecode
  sandbox asked for cursor, google, openai and xai keys, so the operator was being
  asked to approve reach that harness has no way to exercise.
end note

note left of FB
  **Fallback contract:** open-source harnesses (""opencode"", ""cecli"") never set
  ""docker: sbx"" (they take ""docker: dind"") and always run here. Subscription
  harnesses land here only
  when the gate above fails. Def-local shell suites keep covering the fallback
  images; ""tests/e2e/sbx_test.go"" skips unless the host can run sbx.

  **Plan vs proof, and the proof had to be made cheap.** The print-only tests
  assert the ARGV from pure ""sandbox.Spec"" with no VM.
  ""TestEveryHarnessGetsTheDockerAccessItPromises"" runs the harness for real on
  this backend and asserts what ""docker: sbx"" claims: the sbx line was printed
  (not the fallback), the workspace mount carries writes back to the host, and the
  sandbox is gone afterwards. It drives ""--shell"", so it costs no model call and
  no credential.

  **That test alone was not enough, and the gap is instructive.** Every adapter
  defect — a rejected ""-w"", a ""-v"" that is ""--cloud"" only, an image in the agent
  positional, an agent name that must match the Kit's, an unparseable Kit, a stale
  template served silently — was found by running ""proveo run"" BY HAND. The real
  run failed at each of them but reported only //"session exited before the
  sandbox shell"// six minutes later, naming none. Three assertions close that:

  | ""TestSandboxKitValidatesAgainstTheRealCLI"" | renders the Kit and hands it to ""sbx kit validate"" — catches schema drift in ~2s with no VM, and treats a DEPRECATION warning as failure because that is sbx announcing the schema moved |
  | ""TestSandboxKitDeclaresOnlyPermittedProviders"" | the Kit may only request credentials the manifest permits |
  | the run probe | fails on sbx's own ""ERROR:"" line, and on an interactive prompt, instead of waiting out the clock |

  A fast pass is where a vacuous assertion hides, so the validator was checked
  against a deliberately broken Kit: at ""schemaVersion: 1"" it fails in 1.7s naming
  //"field permissions not found in type spec\.SpecFile"//. Its own first version
  had a wrong glob — watching ""<state>/<sid>/"" while the Kit lands at
  ""<state>/egress/<sid>/"" — and spent three minutes timing out on a file already
  written one directory over. It failed loudly rather than passing, which is the
  only reason that was cheap.
end note
note bottom of SEL
  **proveo owns the install and the version, not the operator.** ""sbx"" is
  pre-GA and its surface moves, so "installed" is not the same as "drivable":
  ""sbx\.MinVersion"" is the surface this build targets, ""Available()"" refuses
  anything older **at selection time**, and the run falls back to docker+egress
  with the reason named. Every drift the pin exists for fails deep inside a
  sandbox otherwise, where the operator cannot see it.

  ""ensureSbx"" then offers to close the gap — ""InstallCmd(installed)"" returns
  the //install// line when the CLI is absent and the //upgrade// line when it is
  merely old, because handing "brew install" to someone who installed it
  yesterday is how a version gate becomes advice they follow twice and then
  ignore. The install is **confirmed, never silent**: it mutates the host outside
  proveo's own state, so it takes the same gate as a missing sidecar image
  (""PROVEO_AUTO_PROVISION"", else a TTY prompt, else declined — and declining is
  a fallback, not an error).

  **""--print"" provisions nothing.** A dry run is how an operator inspects a
  posture before committing to it; if it could install a package manager's worth
  of software the flag would stop being safe to reach for. ""sbxReady"" splits on
  exactly that.
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **Measured against v0.39, flag by flag, by running it.** What the adapter was
  written for (v0.35) and what the CLI takes differ on every axis that matters,
  and each one failed a real run in turn:

  | workspaces | ""-v host:container"" → **positional** ""PATH[:ro]"" (""--volume"" is ""--cloud"" only) |
  | image | positional → ""-t/--template""; the first positional is the AGENT |
  | agent | **must equal the Kit's ""name""** — "agent name X does not match agent kit name Y" |
  | agent | and must NOT be a built-in — "agent "cursor" is already registered" ⇒ ""proveo-<target>"" |
  | workdir | ""-w"" → **no such flag**; ""PROVEO_WORKDIR"" carries it instead |
  | teardown | ""rm NAME"" → ""rm --force NAME"", or it waits for a confirmation |
  | secrets | ""secret set NAME"" → ""--force"", or a re-run's piped value answers the overwrite prompt and CANCELS the write |

  **The agent is not a separate thing to declare.** A ""kind: sandbox"" Kit defines
  one, and its name is the agent name — an ""sbxAgent:"" manifest field was added
  for this and then refused by sbx, so it is derived from the target instead.

  **It is NAMESPACED, because sbx also refuses to let a Kit shadow a built-in**
  (claude · codex · copilot · cursor · docker-agent · droid · gemini · kiro ·
  opencode · shell). Two defs are named after built-ins, and the refusal is
  silent from the operator's seat: cursor's runs died ~12s in with the session
  gone before the shell. ""sbx\.AgentName"" prefixes rather than checking the list,
  since the list grows and a collision test would pass until it did.

  **Mounts are path-preserving.** A workspace lands inside the VM at its own HOST
  path over virtiofs, the cwd is that path, and the user is the image's own. There
  is no flag to choose a container path, so proveo home travels as a workspace
  with ""HOME"" repointed at its host path (which keeps ~/.claude and resume state
  across runs) — and a bind NESTED under home, the gh config at
  ""/proveo-home/.config/gh"", cannot be reproduced at all and is dropped.

  **The image store is crossed locally, per user, and identity is what matters.**
  ""docker save -o <tar>"" then ""template load <tar>"" — it takes a FILE, not stdin,
  so the obvious pipe fails before a byte moves. Presence alone is not enough: a
  REBUILT "":latest"" matches by reference and would never reload, which is exactly
  how a sandbox came up with no ""/app"" after the workspace layout was
  standardised and the images rebuilt.

  **Identity comes from a RECEIPT proveo writes, not from the store's IMAGE ID
  column**, because ""sbx create"" re-bakes the template and rewrites that column:
  one create turns ""2ca281d785dc"" into ""5fcb2266417f"" under the same reference.
  Comparing against it mismatches forever after the first run and re-loads a
  multi-GB tar every launch. Full measurements, and the repair that the removed
  reload used to provide by accident, are in
  ""_spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox\.puml"".

  **A workspace must be a DIRECTORY** — "workspace path exists but is not a
  directory". File binds are dropped, so the project ""\.env"" and the
  ""/dev/null"" masks over it do not reach an sbx sandbox.
end note
note bottom of KIT
  **The Kit has TWO schema versions, and ""schemaVersion"" selects the SHAPE of two
  blocks — not just a compatibility marker.**

  | | v1 | v2 (what this package writes) |
  | allowlist | ""network.allowedDomains"" | ""permissions.network.allow"" |
  | entrypoint | a mapping (""{run: […]}"") | a plain list |

  Mixing them fails validation rather than degrading: ""permissions"" under v1 is
  "field not found", and a mapping entrypoint under v2 is "cannot unmarshal !!map
  into []string". v1 still validates with a deprecation warning naming v2.

  //Two earlier claims here were wrong and are corrected by this.// The first said
  ""permissions.network"" //replaces// ""network.allowedDomains"" — taken from the
  published docs, which describe v2 while the binary rejects ""permissions"" at v1.
  The second said ""network.allowedDomains"" was right all along. Both are real;
  the version chooses. ""sbx kit validate"" settled it, and is the only thing that
  could have.

  What v0.39 actually rejected in the shipped Kit was narrower than either claim:
  ""image"" (belongs under ""sandbox"") and ""credentialsEnv"" (replaced by
  ""credentials[]"") are not fields of ""spec\.SpecFile"" at all.

  **""sandbox.entrypoint"" is read from the IMAGE**, not restated per def
  (""docker image inspect … .Config.Entrypoint""): the two would drift the moment a
  def changed its entrypoint, and nothing would notice until a sandbox ran the
  wrong command. It has to be declared because sbx's own agent command would
  otherwise replace it — and that entrypoint is where the seeded guide file, the
  model-alias bridging, the LSP wiring and the subagent set come from.
end note

note bottom of SEC
  **The Kit's credential block IS proveo's broker, declared instead of
  implemented.** ""credentials[].apiKey"" carries ""proxyManaged: true"" so the value
  never enters the VM, and ""inject: [{domain, header, format}]"" names the
  destination and header it may ride — the same on-route rule
  ""internal/broker"" enforces for the docker path, and the convergence the
  experiment note predicted.

  **Derived from the SECRETS being injected, not from the detected providers.**
  Those are different sets: claudecode's ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" is
  manifest-declared and injected whether or not detection saw it, so keying on
  detection left the Kit silently empty — declaring no brokering while proveo went
  on to broker. A unit test pins it.

  **One entry per service.** anthropic accepts either credential and an operator
  may hold both; declaring the service twice leaves the effective one to map order
  inside sbx. First wins, and the order is meaningful — manifest-declared secrets
  precede detected keys, so the credential the harness announces is the one that
  stands.

  No value is written into the spec: the env var names it, the header says where
  it may go, and the secret travels only over ""sbx secret set"" on stdin.
  ""--token"" would put it on an argv, which the credential boundary forbids.
end note
note bottom of SBX
  **One pinned CLI surface.** ""sbx"" is pre-GA (v0.35), so every flag it takes
  is encoded in exactly four places — ""RunArgs"", ""RemoveArgs"",
  ""SecretSetArgs"", ""WriteKit"" — and callers stay declarative. Drift against
  the tool then costs one edit in one package rather than a sweep through
  ""cmd/proveo"".

  Reference docs:
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/""        | platform gate + standalone install
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/usage/""          | run / exec / cp / ports / policy / secret
  | ""https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kits/"" | Kit spec.yaml
end note

note bottom of KIT
  **""WriteKit"" returns the directory, not the file.** ""--kit"" takes a
  directory (or a ZIP), while the rendered document is ""«dir»/spec\.yaml"".
  Returning the path to the file invited exactly one bug — handing ""--kit"" a
  ""spec\.yaml"" — so the function returns the value the flag wants and the
  caller has nothing left to get wrong. The rendered file is ""0600"": it holds
  no secret values, but the posture it declares is private.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **Purity split.** ""sandbox.Spec"" is a pure function of ""sandbox.Input"":
  same input, same Kit + argv + secret list, no side effects — which is what
  makes ""--print"" and the unit tests able to assert the whole invocation
  without a sandbox. Every effect lives in ""sandbox.Run"": render the Kit,
  hydrate the forwarded host env, ""sbx secret set"", exec, then ""sbx rm"" the
  VM, its images and its volumes on the way out.
end note

note right of SEC
  **Credential posture follows the harness, not the backend.**

  | ""--credentials broker"" (claudecode's default) | ""sbx secret set"" host-side; the name is listed in the Kit's ""credentialsEnv"" and the value never enters argv, the env, or the VM filesystem |
  | ""--credentials forward"" (cursor's only mode)  | a **bare** ""-e NAME"" — ""sbx"" copies the value from the host environment, so it still never rides argv |

  cursor cannot use the brokered path at all: it pins TLS to "".cursor\.sh"" /
  "".cursor\.com"", so header injection has nothing to inject into. Forwarding
  on the sandbox backend therefore has to match what the docker path already
  does for it, or cursor would reach the agent with no usable key — the
  parity break that kept the harness second-class here.
end note

note bottom of SBX
  **A failure with no evidence is itself a diagnosis.**

  The failure handler offers three records of what went wrong, and the first two
  can be legitimately empty at once. An interactive run hands the terminal to the
  child — wrapping stdout would cost the agent its tty — so no tail is taken;
  an agent that dies before its first turn opens no session, so there is no
  transcript to name. What reached the operator was a stopped sandbox and a
  ""kept for diagnosis"" line, which reads as an infrastructure fault and sends
  them into ""sbx exec"" after a cause that was never inside the VM.

  //One of those two emptinesses was a defect rather than a fact.// The transcript
  is written into sandbox-LOCAL volumes, and the copy-out
  (""proveo_sync_state save"") ran on the SUCCESS path only — so
  ""agentTranscript"" searched a host home the failed session had never written
  to, and could never name a transcript however much the agent had said.
  ""saveSandboxState"" now runs on BOTH exits, before the lookup.

  **The sentence that used to stand here was wrong, and both halves cost data.**
  It read: //on a failed run it starts a stopped sandbox and so re-runs the seed's//
  //restore; that is safe for the evidence, because restore is cp -a with no//
  //deletes and the run's own transcript keeps an mtime that postdates startedAt.//

  * ""cp -a"" //has// no deletes and truncates anyway — it opens its destination
    with ""O_TRUNC"". The restart's ""restore"" ran concurrently with this ""save"",
    opposite directions over the same files, and rewrote seven of the operator's
    transcripts short. See ""_spec/internal/sbx/state-sync\.puml"".
  * "an mtime that postdates ""startedAt""" is satisfied by the **restart's own
    artifacts**. One run was handed ""66523790-…jsonl"" as what the agent said:
    zero bytes, created at ""15:12:13.694"" — seventeen seconds after the agent it
    was quoting had died — belonging to no session that ever ran. It won because
    it was the newest ""\.jsonl"" in the home.

  So the window is closed at BOTH ends — ""startedAt"" to ""endedAt"", stamped the
  instant the child returned and before proveo touches anything — and an empty
  file is not evidence. That second rule matters more than it looks: a zero-byte
  transcript satisfied "a transcript exists", which set ""said"" and //suppressed
  the credential hint below//. The run that most needed an explanation got a path
  to an empty file instead.

  **The third record is the run's own log, and the failure line names it.**
  ""PROVEO_HOME/logs"" holds every line the run printed — the resolved posture,
  the credential warnings — and by the time an agent dies those have scrolled off
  a terminal nobody redirected. The macOS run whose login file had blanked tokens
  announced exactly that in its twelfth line, and was diagnosed from scrollback
  that no longer existed. See ""_spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript\.puml"".

  So the empty case is answered rather than left blank. When neither channel
  said anything, proveo asks whether **any** credential reached the agent —
  read off the run's own decision (the rendered env, plus what was written to
  the store, plus a usable login on disk) rather than re-derived, because two
  answers that can disagree will. If none did, it names the variable, the
  command that mints one, and the store step.

  **But the run's own decision cannot see the whole store, and the hint used to
  claim more than it knew.** The credential store is GLOBAL and outlives every
  run, so an entry written last week is injected into a sandbox this run gave
  nothing to. Measured on the failure that exposed it: the stored
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" was live — 200 from ""api.anthropic.com/v1/models"" —
  while proveo's own view said no credential had reached the agent. So
  ""sbx secret ls"" is consulted, and when it lists one of the harness's own
  credentials the hint drops the claim and names a suspect instead: //this run
  sent no credential of its own; the agent had only sbx's stored NAME, whose
  value proveo cannot read.//

  **The trap it exists to name:** ""sbx secret ls"" prints the NAME, so an entry
  holding an EMPTY value looks exactly like a populated one, and proveo cannot
  read the value back to tell them apart. An operator who "already set that"
  had, and it was blank.

  Silent whenever a credential DID arrive — a hint that fires on every failure
  trains the operator to skip the one time it was right.

  **The same store is now read BEFORE the run, not only after it.** Reading it
  only from the post-mortem left proveo refusing runs it could have started —
  see ""_spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary\.puml"".
end note

note as Memory #F4F9FB
  **Half the daemon is a SINGLE-sandbox answer.**

  sbx defaults a sandbox to 50% of HOST memory, which cannot bind wherever
  Docker itself runs in a VM: the limit exceeds the machine it sits in, the
  container grows past what the VM has, and the run dies with a bare SIGKILL
  (137) carrying no OOMKilled flag. ""MemoryLimit"" derives the same 50% from the
  daemon's own ""MemTotal"" instead, which is correct on every platform because on
  native Linux ""MemTotal"" //is// host memory.

  **The share is PER-sandbox, and nothing adds them up.** Each limit binds on its
  own, so N sandboxes ask for N×50% of a machine that has 100% and only the host
  notices. Measured: a 48 GiB host whose daemon reports 23.5 GiB gives ""12031m""
  each — four of those is 47 GiB, and ""--cpus"" is never passed at all, so every
  sandbox also believes it owns all 14 cores.

  So the divisor is stated rather than assumed:
    * ""PROVEO_SBX_INSTANCES"" — how many are meant to coexist. Shares then sum to
      the machine instead of to a multiple of it (n=4 → ""6015m"").
    * ""PROVEO_SBX_MEMORY"" — the cap outright, skipping the derivation entirely.
      It is the only one that works when the daemon cannot be read at all
      (""8g"" → ""8192m"").

  Neither set leaves the historical half exactly as it was: nobody who set
  nothing gets a behaviour change. A malformed value falls BACK to the
  derivation rather than through to sbx — sbx rejects a bad ""-m"" once the run is
  under way, and reports a flag, never the variable behind it.
end note

@enduml
